r">Here's how each piece works:</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>1. Representing categories as embeddings</strong></p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr">For every node in your fixed taxonomy (at every level =E2=80=94 Domai=
n, Field, Subfield), you need a vector to compare documents against. Two wa=
ys to build it, best used together:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [=
&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc =
flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3 print:block print:space-y-1">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Description embedding</strong>: write a 1=E2=80=933 sentence descri=
ption of what belongs in that category (you'll have these from the taxonomy=
-building step), embed it with BGE-M3.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
<strong>Centroid embedding</strong>: average the embeddings of the sample d=
ocuments that ended up in that cluster during taxonomy construction.<br>
Averaging these two (or just concatenating both as reference points and tak=
ing the max similarity) tends to be more robust than either alone =E2=80=94=
 the description captures intent, the centroid captures what real documents=
 in that category actually look like.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>2. Top-down comparison through the hierarchy</strong></p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr">Rather than comparing a new document against every leaf category dire=
ctly (which gets noisy as the taxonomy grows), walk down the tree:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr" class=3D"[li_&amp;]:mb-0 [li_&amp;]:mt-1 [li_&amp;]:gap-1 [=
&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc =
flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3 print:block print:space-y-1">
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Compare against the top-level Domain categories (Science, Economics, etc.) =
first, pick the best match.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Within that Domain, compare against its Field-level children.</li>
<li class=3D"font-claude-response-body whitespace-normal break-words pl-2">=
Continue down until you hit a leaf or the confidence drops.<br>
This is both faster (fewer comparisons per level) and more accurate =E2=80=
=94 it avoids a document about "quantum economics" accidentally matching a =
Physics subfield just because of surface vocabulary overlap.</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>3. Confidence threshold</strong></p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr">At each level, take the cosine similarity to the best-matching catego=
ry and the gap to the second-best. High similarity + a clear gap =E2=86=92 =
auto-assign. Low similarity or a close call between two categories =E2=86=
=92 don't guess, escalate. You'll want to calibrate this threshold empirica=
lly: run it on a batch of documents you already know the right answer for (=
from your taxonomy-construction sample) and pick a cutoff that gives you th=
e error rate you're comfortable with. Expect roughly 80=E2=80=9390% of docu=
ments to auto-assign cleanly once the taxonomy is stable, with the rest goi=
ng to the LLM step.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>4. LLM tie-breaker</strong></p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr">For the ambiguous cases, don't ask the LLM to classify freely =E2=80=
=94 constrain it hard to your fixed taxonomy. Give it the document (or a su=
mmary of it, if long), the names + descriptions of the top 3=E2=80=935 cand=
idate categories from the embedding step, and ask it to pick exactly one or=
 say "none fit well." Constraining the choice set like this is what makes a=
 7B local model reliable enough for this job =E2=80=94 open-ended classific=
ation against hundreds of categories would strain it, but picking among 3=
=E2=80=935 well-described options doesn't.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>5. Human review queue</strong></p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr">If even the LLM flags low confidence (or picks "none fit"), don't for=
ce an assignment =E2=80=94 queue it for a quick manual look in your app's U=
I. This is also your early-warning signal that the taxonomy might be missin=
g a category, which matters since you've decided to keep the taxonomy fixed=
 rather than growing it ad hoc.</p>
<p class=3D"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir=3D=
"ltr"><strong>Batch efficiency note:</strong> for 1000+ docs/month, the emb=
edding-similarity pass is essentially free (matrix multiply against a few h=
undred category vectors, done in bulk). The LLM only runs on the subset tha=
t needs it, which keeps the whole monthly run fast even on modest hardware.=
</p>
